WEBVTT

00:00:03.108 --> 00:00:06.818
Three tricky moments in the “Who are we?” conversation.

00:00:06.868 --> 00:00:09.958
Moment one: someone doesn’t want to share.

00:00:10.008 --> 00:00:12.498
That’s fine, and it should be fine.

00:00:12.548 --> 00:00:16.378
Always make sharing optional: “Share what you’re comfortable with.”

00:00:16.428 --> 00:00:19.218
Pushing people to be vulnerable backfires.

00:00:19.268 --> 00:00:22.658
Most people open up more when they feel they don’t have to.

00:00:22.708 --> 00:00:26.078
Moment two: the answers stay on the surface.

00:00:26.128 --> 00:00:28.958
“You get the best from me when there’s coffee.”

00:00:29.008 --> 00:00:30.738
Humour is a normal first step.

00:00:30.788 --> 00:00:36.598
You can go one level deeper by asking: “And when it really matters, what helps you do your best work?”

00:00:36.648 --> 00:00:40.318
Or share a deeper answer of your own, and let the room follow.

00:00:40.368 --> 00:00:43.518
Moment three: something comes up that hurts.

00:00:43.568 --> 00:00:49.762
Someone says, “I struggle when decisions are made in rooms I’m not in,” and everyone knows who they mean.

00:00:49.812 --> 00:00:52.962
Don’t ignore it, and don’t turn it into a confrontation.

00:00:53.012 --> 00:00:55.442
Acknowledge it: “That sounds important.

00:00:55.492 --> 00:00:58.642
Let’s make sure we come back to it in the decisions conversation.”

00:00:58.692 --> 00:01:00.642
Then do come back to it.

00:01:00.692 --> 00:01:03.662
One more thing about the skills marketplace, if you use it.

00:01:03.712 --> 00:01:09.522
It works best when you end with a concrete pairing: who learns what from whom in the next few weeks.

00:01:09.572 --> 00:01:12.382
Without that, it stays a nice list on a wall.

00:01:12.432 --> 00:01:15.522
And remember: this conversation is never done.

00:01:15.572 --> 00:01:20.422
Every time someone joins or leaves, the answer to “who are we?” changes.

00:01:20.472 --> 00:01:21.853
That’s a good moment to repeat it.

